```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'product-card',
  template: `
    <article>
      <h2>{{ name }}</h2>
      <button [disabled]="!inStock" (click)="buy()">Buy</button>
      <p>{{ message }}</p>
    </article>
  `,
})
class ProductCard {
  name = 'Desk lamp';
  inStock = true;
  message = '';
  buy() { this.message = `${this.name} added to the basket`; }
}
```

Angular template bindings connect component data to the rendered view. Interpolation, written as `{{ expression }}`, is convenient for text. Property binding, written as `[disabled]="isSaving"`, evaluates an expression and assigns the result to a DOM element, directive, or child-component property. This preserves types, so a boolean remains a boolean instead of becoming the string `"false"`.

Event binding uses parentheses, such as `(click)="save()"`. Angular registers a listener and evaluates the statement when the event occurs. The special `$event` value contains the event payload. Two-way binding uses the banana-in-a-box syntax `[(...)]`. With form controls, `[(ngModel)]` combines the current `ngModel` value with its change event.

For example:

```typescript
@Component({
  imports: [FormsModule],
  template: `<input [(ngModel)]="name">
             <button [disabled]="!name" (click)="save()">Save</button>
             <p>Hello {{ name }}</p>`,
})
class Editor { name = ''; save() {} }
```

Two-way binding is useful when a control and component state truly represent one value, but it can hide the two directions in more complex data flows. Explicit property and event bindings are often clearer between components. The main distinction is direction: interpolation and property binding move data to the view, event binding moves a notification to the class, and two-way binding deliberately combines both.

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'profile-editor',
  imports: [FormsModule],
  template: `
    <label>Name <input [(ngModel)]="name"></label>
    <button [disabled]="name.trim().length === 0" (click)="save()">Save</button>
    <p [attr.aria-label]="'Preview for ' + name">Preview: {{ name }}</p>
  `,
})
class ProfileEditor {
  name = 'Asha';
  save() { console.log('Saved', this.name); }
}
```

Inputs and outputs define a component's public data contract. An input lets a parent supply a value to a child. In current Angular, `input()` creates an input signal and `input.required<T>()` marks a value as required. The child reads it by calling the signal. The older `@Input` decorator remains supported in existing code.

An output represents an event chosen by the component author. `output<T>()` creates a typed emitter. The child calls `emit(value)`, and the parent handles that event with normal Angular event-binding syntax. Custom outputs do not bubble through the DOM, so only the template that binds the output receives it.

For example:

```typescript
interface User { id: number; name: string; }

class UserCard {
  user = input.required<User>();
  selected = output<number>();
  choose() { this.selected.emit(this.user().id); }
}
// Parent template: <user-card [user]="person" (selected)="open($event)" />
```

The usual pattern is data down and events up. The parent owns the selected user, passes each record down, and decides what a selection means. A child should not mutate an input object merely to change parent state, because that hides ownership and can also cause stale-reference problems. For tightly coupled controls, a model input can support two-way binding, but ordinary inputs and outputs make most component boundaries easier to understand and test.

```typescript
import { Component, input, output } from '@angular/core';

interface User { id: number; name: string; }

@Component({
  selector: 'user-card',
  template: `<button (click)="choose()">Open {{ user().name }}</button>`,
})
class UserCard {
  readonly user = input.required<User>();
  readonly selected = output<number>();
  choose() { this.selected.emit(this.user().id); }
}

@Component({
  selector: 'user-list',
  imports: [UserCard],
  template: `
    <user-card [user]="person" (selected)="open($event)" />
    <p>Selected ID: {{ selectedId }}</p>
  `,
})
class UserList {
  person: User = { id: 7, name: 'Mina' };
  selectedId: number | null = null;
  open(id: number) { this.selectedId = id; }
}
```
